// BUGS #112: `0..5` must be a parse error, not a running program.
//
// Saffron has no range syntax. There is no Range token, no Range AST node and no
// range codegen — `grep -n "Range\|DotDot"` finds nothing in lexer.sf, parser.sf
// or ast.sf. Asking for a range is therefore a syntax error, and this file's job
// is to prove the compiler says so.
//
// It used to live in test/pass/, where it was asserting a feature that has never
// existed. What actually happened was worse than a wrong answer: the number
// scanner consumed the first `.` as a decimal point, so `0..5` came out as
// TkFloat("0.") TkDot TkInt(5), and parse_call's numeric-dot branch — the one
// that exists for tuple access, `tuple.0` — built IndexGet(FloatLit(0.),
// IntLit(5)). Codegen then emitted
//
//     %t2 = call i64 @__val_tag_float(double 1.)
//     %t7 = call i64 @__list_length(i64 %t2)
//     %t10 = call i64 @__list_get(i64 %t2, i64 %t9)
//
// passing a double to the list helpers. The only output from any stage was
// "[checker] Warning: nums: cannot infer type" — no lexer, parser, checker or
// codegen diagnostic named the real problem. At run time `0..5` raised
// "Runtime Error: NullError: null pointer dereference" and `1..5` segfaulted.
//
// Nothing here is salvageable as a test of something that exists: every
// statement is a range literal. The working equivalent is a C-style or
// list-based loop, which test/pass/ already covers.
//
// Expected: three "'..' is not an operator" errors and exit 1. If this file ever
// compiles cleanly again, either range syntax was added — in which case this
// test should move back and be rewritten — or the guard regressed and `..` is
// silently a float-indexed list read again.

var nums = 0..5
IO.println(nums)
IO.println(nums.length())

for (i in 1..4) {
    IO.println(i)
}

// Reverse range
var rev = 5..0
IO.println(rev)
